WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:07.116 --> 00:09.206 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference July 31,

00:09.206 --> 00:12.196 align:middle
2019 CHAIR POWELL.

00:12.266 --> 00:14.086 align:middle
Good afternoon, and welcome.

00:14.486 --> 00:16.856 align:middle
We decided today
to lower the target

00:16.856 --> 00:20.796 align:middle
for the federal funds rate by
¼ percentage point to a range

00:20.946 --> 00:22.716 align:middle
of 2 percent to 2¼ percent.

00:23.956 --> 00:26.556 align:middle
The outlook for the U.S.
economy remains favorable,

00:27.056 --> 00:29.336 align:middle
and this action is designed
to support that outlook.

00:30.706 --> 00:33.216 align:middle
It is intended to insure
against downside risks

00:33.276 --> 00:36.066 align:middle
from weak global growth and
trade policy uncertainty,

00:36.486 --> 00:39.396 align:middle
to help offset the effects these
factors are currently having

00:39.396 --> 00:43.196 align:middle
on the economy, and to promote
a faster return of inflation

00:43.196 --> 00:45.166 align:middle
to our symmetric 2
percent objective.

00:46.816 --> 00:48.786 align:middle
All of these objectives
will support achievement

00:48.786 --> 00:51.746 align:middle
of our overarching goal:
to sustain the expansion,

00:51.746 --> 00:53.836 align:middle
with a strong job market
and inflation close

00:53.836 --> 00:56.456 align:middle
to our objective, for the
benefit of the American people.

00:58.366 --> 01:00.146 align:middle
We also decided to
conclude the runoff

01:00.146 --> 01:03.406 align:middle
of our securities portfolio in
August rather than in September,

01:03.406 --> 01:04.426 align:middle
as previously planned.

01:04.926 --> 01:07.836 align:middle
And I'll discuss the thinking
behind today's interest rate

01:07.836 --> 01:09.826 align:middle
reduction and then turn
to the path forward.

01:10.686 --> 01:14.346 align:middle
As the year began,
both the economy

01:14.346 --> 01:16.486 align:middle
and monetary policy
were in a good place.

01:17.466 --> 01:19.276 align:middle
The unemployment rate
was below 4 percent,

01:19.276 --> 01:21.946 align:middle
and inflation had been running
near our 2 percent objective

01:21.946 --> 01:22.866 align:middle
for nine months.

01:23.976 --> 01:25.896 align:middle
Our interest rate
target was at the low end

01:25.896 --> 01:26.956 align:middle
of estimates of neutral.

01:27.716 --> 01:30.366 align:middle
Over the first half of
the year, the economy grew

01:30.366 --> 01:33.566 align:middle
at a healthy pace and job
gains pushed unemployment

01:33.596 --> 01:35.486 align:middle
to near a half-century low.

01:36.786 --> 01:38.376 align:middle
Wages have been rising,
particularly

01:38.376 --> 01:39.586 align:middle
for lower-paying jobs.

01:40.496 --> 01:41.986 align:middle
People who live and work in low-

01:41.986 --> 01:44.346 align:middle
and middle-income
communities tell us that many

01:44.346 --> 01:47.116 align:middle
who have struggled to find work
are now getting opportunities

01:47.596 --> 01:49.976 align:middle
to add new and better
chapters to their lives.

01:51.536 --> 01:53.246 align:middle
This underscores for
us the importance

01:53.246 --> 01:54.556 align:middle
of sustaining the expansion

01:54.556 --> 01:56.496 align:middle
so that the strong job
market reaches more

01:56.496 --> 01:57.506 align:middle
of those left behind.

01:59.236 --> 02:01.746 align:middle
Through the course of the
year, weak global growth,

02:01.746 --> 02:03.146 align:middle
trade policy uncertainty,

02:03.146 --> 02:05.526 align:middle
and muted inflation
have prompted the FOMC

02:05.526 --> 02:06.806 align:middle
to adjust its assessment

02:06.806 --> 02:08.636 align:middle
of the appropriate
path of interest rates.

02:09.726 --> 02:10.496 align:middle
The Committee moved

02:10.496 --> 02:13.776 align:middle
from expecting rate increases
this year to a patient stance

02:13.776 --> 02:16.456 align:middle
about any changes and
then to today's action.

02:18.016 --> 02:20.016 align:middle
The median Committee
participant's assessments

02:20.016 --> 02:22.506 align:middle
of the neutral rate of interest
and the longer-run normal rate

02:22.506 --> 02:24.506 align:middle
of unemployment have
also declined this year,

02:25.166 --> 02:26.236 align:middle
reinforcing the case

02:26.236 --> 02:28.476 align:middle
for a somewhat lower
path for our policy rate.

02:30.726 --> 02:32.656 align:middle
These changes in
the anticipated path

02:32.656 --> 02:34.656 align:middle
of interest rates have
eased financial conditions

02:34.656 --> 02:35.936 align:middle
and have supported the economy.

02:36.496 --> 02:41.066 align:middle
At our June meeting, many
Committee participants saw

02:41.116 --> 02:43.176 align:middle
that the case for lowering
the federal funds rate had

02:43.176 --> 02:46.246 align:middle
strengthened, but the Committee
wanted to get a better sense

02:46.246 --> 02:48.156 align:middle
of the overall direction
of events.

02:49.466 --> 02:51.266 align:middle
Since then, we have
seen both positive

02:51.266 --> 02:52.386 align:middle
and negative developments.

02:53.476 --> 02:55.276 align:middle
Job growth was strong
in June and,

02:55.336 --> 02:57.976 align:middle
looking through month-to-month
fluctuations, the data point

02:57.976 --> 02:59.026 align:middle
to continued strength.

03:00.196 --> 03:02.406 align:middle
We expect job growth to
be slower than last year

03:02.406 --> 03:03.996 align:middle
but above what we
believe is required

03:03.996 --> 03:05.516 align:middle
to hold the unemployment
rate steady.

03:07.816 --> 03:11.026 align:middle
GDP growth in the second quarter
came in close to expectations.

03:11.816 --> 03:14.086 align:middle
Consumption-supported
by rising incomes

03:14.086 --> 03:17.196 align:middle
and high household confidence-is
the main engine driving the

03:17.196 --> 03:18.056 align:middle
economy forward.

03:18.586 --> 03:22.136 align:middle
But manufacturing
output has declined

03:22.136 --> 03:23.446 align:middle
for two consecutive quarters,

03:23.446 --> 03:25.966 align:middle
and business fixed investment
fell in the second quarter.

03:26.846 --> 03:29.756 align:middle
Foreign growth has disappointed,
particularly in manufacturing

03:29.796 --> 03:31.846 align:middle
and notably in the
euro area and China.

03:33.426 --> 03:35.656 align:middle
In response to this
weakness, many central banks

03:35.656 --> 03:38.356 align:middle
around the world are
increasing policy accommodation

03:38.356 --> 03:39.826 align:middle
or contemplating doing so.

03:41.256 --> 03:42.816 align:middle
After simmering early
in the year,

03:42.856 --> 03:46.586 align:middle
trade policy tensions nearly
boiled over in May and June

03:47.166 --> 03:49.116 align:middle
but now appear to have
returned to a simmer.

03:50.616 --> 03:51.906 align:middle
Looking through this
variability,

03:51.906 --> 03:53.506 align:middle
our business contacts tell us

03:53.506 --> 03:56.396 align:middle
that the ongoing uncertainty
is making some companies more

03:56.396 --> 03:57.996 align:middle
cautious about their
capital spending.

03:59.376 --> 04:02.216 align:middle
The domestic inflation
shortfall has continued.

04:03.476 --> 04:06.776 align:middle
Core inflation, which
excludes food and energy prices

04:06.776 --> 04:08.596 align:middle
and is a better gauge
of future developments

04:08.596 --> 04:11.796 align:middle
than is total inflation,
has run at 1.6 percent

04:11.796 --> 04:12.966 align:middle
over the past 12 months.

04:13.706 --> 04:15.816 align:middle
We continue to expect
that inflation will return

04:15.816 --> 04:17.036 align:middle
over time to 2 percent.

04:17.606 --> 04:20.996 align:middle
But domestic inflation
pressures remain muted,

04:21.626 --> 04:24.446 align:middle
and global disinflationary
pressures persist.

04:26.026 --> 04:27.906 align:middle
Wages are rising,
but not at a pace

04:27.906 --> 04:30.206 align:middle
that would put much upward
pressure on inflation.

04:30.796 --> 04:33.796 align:middle
We are mindful that
inflation's return

04:33.796 --> 04:35.476 align:middle
to 2 percent may
be further delayed,

04:36.156 --> 04:38.556 align:middle
and that continued below-target
inflation could lead

04:38.556 --> 04:39.406 align:middle
to a worrisome

04:39.406 --> 04:41.796 align:middle
and difficult-to-reverse
downward slide

04:41.796 --> 04:43.246 align:middle
in longer-term expectations.

04:44.426 --> 04:47.396 align:middle
So taking all of that on board,

04:47.536 --> 04:49.886 align:middle
the Committee still sees a
favorable baseline outlook.

04:50.656 --> 04:54.336 align:middle
Over the year, however, incoming
information on global growth,

04:54.566 --> 04:55.866 align:middle
trade policy uncertainty,

04:55.866 --> 04:58.006 align:middle
and muted inflation
have led the Committee

04:58.006 --> 05:00.346 align:middle
to gradually lower its
assessments of the path

05:00.346 --> 05:03.796 align:middle
of policy interest rate that
would best support that outlook.

05:04.556 --> 05:07.606 align:middle
Today, we judge that those
factors warrant the policy

05:07.606 --> 05:11.746 align:middle
adjustment I described: "As
the Committee contemplates the

05:11.746 --> 05:14.596 align:middle
future path of the target range
for the federal funds rate,

05:15.826 --> 05:17.476 align:middle
it will continue to
monitor the implications

05:17.476 --> 05:20.376 align:middle
of incoming information for the
economic outlook and will act

05:20.376 --> 05:22.136 align:middle
as appropriate to
sustain the expansion,

05:22.136 --> 05:23.156 align:middle
with a strong labor market

05:23.156 --> 05:25.186 align:middle
and inflation near its
symmetric 2 percent objective."

05:25.686 --> 05:26.036 align:middle
Thank you.

05:26.036 --> 05:27.336 align:middle
I am happy to take
your questions.

05:28.186 --> 05:29.536 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

05:30.976 --> 05:33.466 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

05:33.466 --> 05:36.026 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek with
the New York Times.

05:36.286 --> 05:39.946 align:middle
Per your statement here,
I guess the question is,

05:40.636 --> 05:42.726 align:middle
is there any reason to believe

05:42.726 --> 05:45.986 align:middle
that a 25 basis point cut
is going to be sufficient

05:46.096 --> 05:49.706 align:middle
to expediently return inflation
to your 2 percent target?

05:50.246 --> 05:53.536 align:middle
And, if not, what are you going
to be looking at to convince you

05:53.966 --> 05:55.186 align:middle
that you need to
cut rates again?

05:55.186 --> 05:56.476 align:middle
What is the hurdle rate there?

05:57.006 --> 05:57.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

05:57.416 --> 06:00.866 align:middle
So I think you have to look at
not just the 25 basis point cut,

06:01.356 --> 06:02.716 align:middle
but look at the Committee's
actions

06:02.716 --> 06:03.826 align:middle
over the course of the year.

06:03.956 --> 06:06.006 align:middle
As I noted in my
opening statement,

06:07.356 --> 06:10.146 align:middle
we started off expecting
some rate increases,

06:10.146 --> 06:12.116 align:middle
we moved to a patient
setting for a few months,

06:12.116 --> 06:12.966 align:middle
and now we've moved here.

06:12.966 --> 06:15.966 align:middle
I think what you've seen over
the course of the year is,

06:15.966 --> 06:17.956 align:middle
as we've moved to a more
accommodative policy,

06:17.956 --> 06:22.036 align:middle
the economy has actually
performed just about as expected

06:22.196 --> 06:24.716 align:middle
with that gradually
increasing support.

06:24.716 --> 06:26.736 align:middle
And I think-I wouldn't take
credit for all of that,

06:26.736 --> 06:30.086 align:middle
but I do think that increasing
policy support has kept the

06:30.086 --> 06:33.736 align:middle
economy on track and kept
the outlook favorable.

06:34.096 --> 06:37.906 align:middle
In terms of the rest
of your question,

06:38.356 --> 06:42.416 align:middle
the Committee is really
thinking of this as a way

06:42.416 --> 06:43.526 align:middle
of adjusting policy

06:43.526 --> 06:45.576 align:middle
to a somewhat more
accommodative stance

06:46.046 --> 06:48.596 align:middle
to further the three
objectives that I mentioned:

06:49.296 --> 06:53.786 align:middle
to insure against downside
risks, to provide support

06:53.786 --> 06:59.726 align:middle
to the economy, that those
factors are-where factors are

06:59.726 --> 07:02.756 align:middle
pushing down on economic growth,
and then to support inflation.

07:02.756 --> 07:05.586 align:middle
So we do think it will serve
all of those goals, but again,

07:05.586 --> 07:07.696 align:middle
we're thinking of it as
essentially in the nature

07:07.696 --> 07:09.526 align:middle
of a midcycle adjustment
to policy.

07:11.616 --> 07:15.746 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

07:17.716 --> 07:20.156 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

07:20.436 --> 07:22.856 align:middle
There's a perception out there
that perhaps, in this case,

07:22.856 --> 07:25.226 align:middle
the Fed is something of a
hammer in search of a nail,

07:25.296 --> 07:27.546 align:middle
because the latest
consumer spending reports,

07:27.546 --> 07:33.016 align:middle
as you suggested, don't seem to
show any kind of demand problem

07:33.066 --> 07:35.806 align:middle
in the U.S. And when you
look at mortgage rates,

07:35.806 --> 07:38.176 align:middle
auto lending rates,
they've all come down.

07:38.586 --> 07:44.876 align:middle
And so, wondering exactly what
problem lower capital costs

07:45.226 --> 07:46.056 align:middle
will solve.

07:46.816 --> 07:47.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:47.306 --> 07:49.286 align:middle
So, you're absolutely right.

07:49.286 --> 07:50.696 align:middle
The-the performance

07:50.696 --> 07:52.536 align:middle
of the economy has
been reasonably good.

07:53.136 --> 07:57.276 align:middle
The position of the economy
is as close to our objectives

07:57.276 --> 08:00.496 align:middle
as it's been in a long time,
and the outlook is also good.

08:00.706 --> 08:03.406 align:middle
What we've been monitoring since
the beginning of the year is,

08:03.406 --> 08:05.876 align:middle
effectively, downside
risks to that outlook

08:06.346 --> 08:09.246 align:middle
from weakening global growth,
and we see that everywhere:

08:09.616 --> 08:11.996 align:middle
weak manufacturing, weak
global growth now, particularly

08:11.996 --> 08:14.786 align:middle
in the European Union and China.

08:14.786 --> 08:17.486 align:middle
In addition, we see trade
policy developments,

08:17.486 --> 08:19.206 align:middle
which at times have
been disruptive

08:19.206 --> 08:20.396 align:middle
and then have been less so,

08:20.626 --> 08:23.066 align:middle
and also inflation
running below target.

08:23.066 --> 08:25.356 align:middle
So we see those as threats

08:25.356 --> 08:28.966 align:middle
to what is clearly a favorable
outlook, and we see this action

08:28.966 --> 08:32.256 align:middle
as designed to support them and
keep that outlook favorable.

08:32.366 --> 08:34.486 align:middle
And, frankly, it
is a continuation

08:34.486 --> 08:37.936 align:middle
of what we've been doing all
year to provide more support

08:38.466 --> 08:40.666 align:middle
against those very same risks.

08:40.876 --> 08:41.186 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

08:41.186 --> 08:43.976 align:middle
But the follow-up question
is, how does it do that?

08:44.046 --> 08:47.426 align:middle
How does cutting
interest rates lower-or,

08:47.426 --> 08:50.366 align:middle
how does cutting interest rates
keep that going, since the cost

08:50.366 --> 08:52.966 align:middle
of capital doesn't seem
to be the issue here?

08:52.966 --> 08:53.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:53.236 --> 08:55.926 align:middle
You know, I-I really think it
does, and I think the evidence

08:56.016 --> 09:00.176 align:middle
of my eyes tells me that our
policy does support-it supports

09:00.176 --> 09:03.346 align:middle
confidence, it supports
economic activity, household

09:03.346 --> 09:04.456 align:middle
and business confidence,

09:04.456 --> 09:06.216 align:middle
and through channels
that we understand.

09:06.216 --> 09:08.026 align:middle
So it will lower
borrowing costs.

09:08.506 --> 09:10.436 align:middle
It will-and it will work.

09:10.436 --> 09:11.376 align:middle
And I think you see it.

09:11.376 --> 09:16.036 align:middle
Since-you know, since
we noted our vigilance

09:16.036 --> 09:17.386 align:middle
about the situation in June,

09:17.386 --> 09:19.686 align:middle
you saw financial
conditions move up,

09:19.946 --> 09:22.316 align:middle
and you saw-I won't take
credit for the whole recovery,

09:22.316 --> 09:24.066 align:middle
but you saw financial
conditions move up.

09:24.366 --> 09:26.136 align:middle
You see confidence,
which had troughed

09:26.666 --> 09:28.216 align:middle
in June-you saw it move back up.

09:28.216 --> 09:31.796 align:middle
You see economic activity
on a healthy basis.

09:31.796 --> 09:34.366 align:middle
It just-it seems to work through
confidence channels as well

09:34.366 --> 09:38.646 align:middle
as the mechanical channels
that you are talking about.

09:40.046 --> 09:41.186 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

09:42.196 --> 09:47.126 align:middle
Hi, Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

09:47.886 --> 09:50.226 align:middle
You always say that the
Fed is data dependent,

09:50.226 --> 09:52.056 align:middle
and much of the data
that we've seen

09:52.116 --> 09:54.966 align:middle
since the June meeting has
surprised to the upside

09:54.966 --> 09:57.576 align:middle
or at least been in
line with expectations.

09:57.926 --> 09:59.516 align:middle
Can you give us a sense of how

09:59.516 --> 10:04.326 align:middle
that better-than-expected data
impacted the FOMC's thinking?

10:04.326 --> 10:07.066 align:middle
And if we keep seeing
these upside surprises,

10:07.616 --> 10:11.846 align:middle
does that change or evolve any
FOMC thinking going forward?

10:12.036 --> 10:12.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:12.436 --> 10:15.246 align:middle
So, yes. I mean, I think
we-of course, you know,

10:15.246 --> 10:20.666 align:middle
what we do at every meeting, as
I noted, is, we do a deep dive

10:20.666 --> 10:25.606 align:middle
into U.S. economic activity and
global activity and, certainly,

10:25.726 --> 10:28.266 align:middle
carefully went through
U.S. economic activity,

10:28.266 --> 10:30.196 align:middle
which there has been some
positive and some negative, but,

10:30.196 --> 10:32.926 align:middle
overall, the U.S. economy has
shown resilience during the

10:32.926 --> 10:33.926 align:middle
intermeeting period.

10:34.576 --> 10:39.226 align:middle
But, again, the-the issue
is more the downside risks

10:39.226 --> 10:40.616 align:middle
and then the shortfall
in inflation,

10:40.616 --> 10:42.846 align:middle
and we're trying
to address those.

10:43.476 --> 10:49.616 align:middle
So, in addition, going
forward, I would say,

10:49.616 --> 10:51.936 align:middle
we're going to be
monitoring those same things.

10:51.936 --> 10:55.916 align:middle
We'll be monitoring the
evolution of trade uncertainty,

10:55.916 --> 10:58.136 align:middle
of global growth,
and of low inflation.

10:58.136 --> 10:58.976 align:middle
And we'll also, of course,

10:58.976 --> 11:01.216 align:middle
be watching the performance
of the U.S. economy.

11:01.216 --> 11:04.256 align:middle
As I mentioned, it had
shown some resilience here

11:04.256 --> 11:06.556 align:middle
to those issues, and we'll be
putting all of that together,

11:06.556 --> 11:09.396 align:middle
and that's how we'll be thinking
about policy going forward.

11:11.286 --> 11:12.066 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

11:13.116 --> 11:14.656 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

11:14.826 --> 11:16.916 align:middle
I just want to follow
up on that.

11:17.246 --> 11:20.116 align:middle
Would you say we're sort
of-you guys have gotten

11:20.116 --> 11:21.146 align:middle
into a new regime here?

11:21.146 --> 11:25.086 align:middle
This is sort of an insurance cut
and not a data-dependent cut?

11:25.086 --> 11:29.926 align:middle
And are we now more in the
realm of watching headlines

11:29.926 --> 11:33.726 align:middle
of trade talks than we are
watching unemployment rate

11:33.726 --> 11:36.446 align:middle
and inflation numbers,
or-and growth numbers?

11:36.446 --> 11:40.086 align:middle
What-how do we know what
you're going to do next,

11:40.086 --> 11:41.806 align:middle
and why now in this new regime?

11:41.806 --> 11:42.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:42.156 --> 11:44.206 align:middle
Yes. So I gave three
reasons for what we did,

11:44.206 --> 11:47.196 align:middle
and that is to insure against
downside risks to the outlook

11:47.196 --> 11:48.716 align:middle
from weak global growth
and trade tensions.

11:48.716 --> 11:52.626 align:middle
So that is-in a sense, that
is a risk-management point,

11:52.626 --> 11:53.736 align:middle
and that is a bit of insurance.

11:53.776 --> 11:56.236 align:middle
But we also feel like
weak global growth

11:56.236 --> 11:58.706 align:middle
and trade tensions are having
an effect on the U.S. economy.

11:58.706 --> 12:00.466 align:middle
You see it now in
the second quarter:

12:00.466 --> 12:04.546 align:middle
You see weak investment, you see
weak manufacturing-so support

12:04.666 --> 12:09.196 align:middle
demand there and also
to support return

12:09.196 --> 12:10.196 align:middle
of inflation to 2 percent.

12:10.196 --> 12:14.086 align:middle
But there's definitely an
insurance aspect of it.

12:14.086 --> 12:15.086 align:middle
Trade is unusual.

12:15.086 --> 12:18.876 align:middle
We don't-you know, the thing is,
there isn't a lot of experience

12:18.876 --> 12:21.186 align:middle
in responding to
global trade tensions.

12:21.306 --> 12:27.476 align:middle
So it is a-it's something
that we haven't faced before

12:27.476 --> 12:28.766 align:middle
and that we're learning
by doing.

12:28.766 --> 12:30.706 align:middle
And it is not-it's
not exactly the same

12:30.706 --> 12:33.916 align:middle
as watching global growth,
where you see growth weakening,

12:33.916 --> 12:36.296 align:middle
you see central banks and
governments responding

12:36.296 --> 12:38.216 align:middle
with fiscal policy, and you
see growth strengthening,

12:38.216 --> 12:39.276 align:middle
and you see a business cycle.

12:40.186 --> 12:42.406 align:middle
With trade tensions-which
do seem

12:42.406 --> 12:43.916 align:middle
to be having a significant
effect

12:43.916 --> 12:47.556 align:middle
on financial market conditions
and on the economy-they evolve

12:47.556 --> 12:51.186 align:middle
in a different way, and
we have to follow them.

12:51.186 --> 12:52.766 align:middle
And, by the way, I
want to be clear here.

12:53.196 --> 12:55.846 align:middle
We play no role whatsoever
in assessing

12:55.986 --> 12:59.156 align:middle
or evaluating trade
policies other

12:59.156 --> 13:03.386 align:middle
than as-as trade policy
uncertainty has an effect

13:03.386 --> 13:06.606 align:middle
on the U.S. economy in
the short and medium term.

13:06.606 --> 13:08.726 align:middle
We're not in any way
criticizing trade policy.

13:08.726 --> 13:09.736 align:middle
That's really not our job.

13:10.286 --> 13:12.466 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

13:13.516 --> 13:15.416 align:middle
Thank you.

13:15.416 --> 13:16.906 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

13:17.576 --> 13:20.256 align:middle
So, Chair Powell, you and your
colleagues have offered three

13:20.256 --> 13:22.856 align:middle
reasons to cut rates:
a lower neutral rate

13:22.856 --> 13:24.766 align:middle
that may have made policy
a little bit tighter

13:25.226 --> 13:27.246 align:middle
than anticipated,
the global slowdown

13:27.246 --> 13:30.276 align:middle
and the darker risk picture
from the trade tensions,

13:30.596 --> 13:32.646 align:middle
and this desire to
re-center inflation

13:32.646 --> 13:34.136 align:middle
and inflation expectations.

13:34.566 --> 13:36.606 align:middle
I wonder, which of
those factors, if any,

13:36.606 --> 13:37.816 align:middle
weighs most heavily on you?

13:37.816 --> 13:41.946 align:middle
And, more importantly, is
a ¼ point cut really going

13:42.026 --> 13:46.236 align:middle
to address all of that,
let alone any one of those?

13:47.336 --> 13:47.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:48.216 --> 13:51.106 align:middle
So I actually think different
people have different-different

13:51.106 --> 13:53.236 align:middle
weightings has been my
experience on those things.

13:53.236 --> 13:55.526 align:middle
You mentioned lower r*,

13:55.916 --> 14:02.666 align:middle
trade slowdown-and I would
actually add, lower natural rate

14:02.666 --> 14:05.926 align:middle
of unemployment too has moved
down, all of which kind of point

14:05.926 --> 14:07.906 align:middle
to more accommodation.

14:08.466 --> 14:10.776 align:middle
So, again, I don't think asking

14:10.776 --> 14:12.756 align:middle
about a ¼ point is
really the right question.

14:12.756 --> 14:14.946 align:middle
I think you have to look back
over the course of the year

14:14.946 --> 14:19.686 align:middle
and see the Committee moving
away from rate increases

14:19.686 --> 14:23.616 align:middle
to a neutral posture
to, now, a rate cut.

14:23.616 --> 14:25.276 align:middle
So I think we've
been providing-and

14:25.276 --> 14:27.136 align:middle
that affects the
forward rate path,

14:27.136 --> 14:28.396 align:middle
and it affects financial
conditions,

14:28.396 --> 14:30.096 align:middle
and financial conditions
affect the economy.

14:30.486 --> 14:33.806 align:middle
You see an economy which is
actually performing pretty well.

14:33.876 --> 14:37.226 align:middle
Growth in the first half of this
year is about the same as it was

14:37.226 --> 14:41.516 align:middle
in all of '18 and actually a
little better than our forecast

14:41.516 --> 14:45.086 align:middle
for growth in 2019-at
the end of 2019.

14:45.086 --> 14:48.576 align:middle
So I think, in a way, that's
monetary policy working.

14:49.346 --> 14:52.716 align:middle
And, again, I wouldn't just
look at the 25 basis point cut

14:52.716 --> 14:54.196 align:middle
as the right question.

14:54.946 --> 14:55.846 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

14:56.236 --> 15:00.336 align:middle
If I could follow up, I
guess in May-in May it seemed

15:00.336 --> 15:02.286 align:middle
as if you were setting a
higher bar to cut rates:

15:02.346 --> 15:04.276 align:middle
There would need to be a
deterioration in the outlook.

15:04.616 --> 15:06.476 align:middle
In June, you seemed to suggest

15:06.476 --> 15:08.736 align:middle
that if the outlook
didn't improve,

15:08.736 --> 15:09.656 align:middle
there might be a rate cut.

15:09.906 --> 15:11.166 align:middle
Where is that bar right now,

15:11.166 --> 15:12.716 align:middle
because I think there's
some confusion

15:12.716 --> 15:15.546 align:middle
about how the Committee
is responding.

15:16.236 --> 15:16.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:16.796 --> 15:21.686 align:middle
Yes. So we, as we noted-we noted
at the bottom of the statement

15:21.946 --> 15:24.226 align:middle
that language, which really says
how we're thinking about it.

15:24.286 --> 15:27.456 align:middle
So it says, as we're
contemplating the future path

15:27.456 --> 15:29.826 align:middle
of the target range for
the federal funds rate,

15:29.976 --> 15:33.166 align:middle
we'll continue to
monitor the implications

15:33.166 --> 15:36.196 align:middle
of incoming information-and
it talks about that language.

15:36.196 --> 15:39.626 align:middle
So, you know, I can't-all I can
tell you is, we'll be looking

15:39.626 --> 15:41.216 align:middle
at weak global growth.

15:41.216 --> 15:45.156 align:middle
We'll be looking very carefully
to see how that's happening.

15:45.226 --> 15:49.026 align:middle
And I think you see-you
learn-every cycle you learn

15:49.026 --> 15:49.716 align:middle
about these things.

15:49.716 --> 15:53.316 align:middle
So we will see whether
growth is picking up,

15:53.316 --> 15:54.246 align:middle
whether it's bottoming out.

15:54.246 --> 15:55.196 align:middle
We'll see that picture.

15:55.546 --> 15:56.646 align:middle
We'll also see on trade.

15:56.796 --> 15:57.456 align:middle
You know, we're going

15:57.456 --> 15:59.026 align:middle
to be seeing-I think
we've learned a lot

15:59.026 --> 15:59.906 align:middle
on trade in this cycle.

15:59.906 --> 16:02.636 align:middle
I think we'll continue to learn
more, and on inflation we will.

16:02.636 --> 16:05.546 align:middle
So, in addition, the U.S.
economy itself is-the

16:05.866 --> 16:07.886 align:middle
performance of the U.S.
economy will enter into that.

16:07.886 --> 16:10.916 align:middle
So I would love to be more
precise, but with trade,

16:10.916 --> 16:14.316 align:middle
it is a factor that we have to
assess in kind of a new way.

16:14.746 --> 16:18.966 align:middle
Those are the things that we'll
be looking at, and-you know,

16:18.966 --> 16:20.276 align:middle
in making our decisions
going forward.

16:21.706 --> 16:26.106 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

16:28.586 --> 16:30.416 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business Network.

16:30.726 --> 16:34.326 align:middle
So a rate hike last December
was seen by some economists

16:34.326 --> 16:36.106 align:middle
and the St. Louis Fed president,

16:36.106 --> 16:37.616 align:middle
James Bullard, as
a step too far.

16:38.176 --> 16:41.096 align:middle
Now the Fed's waited about
seven months for a rate cut.

16:41.406 --> 16:43.516 align:middle
You said today you were
concerned about downside risks.

16:43.756 --> 16:45.836 align:middle
So could some of the
weakness on the business side,

16:45.836 --> 16:48.706 align:middle
with that fixed investment
and the sluggish side

16:48.706 --> 16:51.496 align:middle
on the business side, be
because the Fed waited so long?

16:51.496 --> 16:54.196 align:middle
I want to get your thoughts on
that and talk about why you feel

16:54.196 --> 16:55.316 align:middle
that this nudge is
the right level.

16:56.446 --> 16:56.826 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:56.826 --> 16:59.626 align:middle
Yes. So we don't hear
that from businesses.

17:00.246 --> 17:03.546 align:middle
They don't come in and say,
"We're not investing because,

17:03.546 --> 17:05.286 align:middle
you know, the federal
funds rate is too high."

17:05.286 --> 17:06.926 align:middle
I haven't heard that
from a business.

17:07.326 --> 17:10.726 align:middle
What you hear is that demand
is weak for their products.

17:11.156 --> 17:13.686 align:middle
You see manufacturing being
weak all over the world.

17:14.276 --> 17:16.076 align:middle
Investment-business
investment is weak.

17:16.726 --> 17:19.966 align:middle
And I wouldn't lay all of that
at the door of trade talks.

17:19.966 --> 17:23.436 align:middle
I think there's a-there's a
global business cycle happening

17:23.696 --> 17:25.786 align:middle
with manufacturing
and investment,

17:26.396 --> 17:28.916 align:middle
and that's-that's
been, you know,

17:29.376 --> 17:31.816 align:middle
definitely a bigger
factor than, certainly,

17:31.816 --> 17:33.706 align:middle
we expected late last year.

17:33.706 --> 17:36.336 align:middle
I think global-global
growth started to slow

17:36.336 --> 17:39.456 align:middle
down in the middle of last
year, but that has gone

17:39.556 --> 17:40.656 align:middle
on to a greater extent.

17:40.656 --> 17:43.266 align:middle
And, by the way, trade policy
uncertainty has also been,

17:43.636 --> 17:45.206 align:middle
I think, more elevated
than we anticipated.

17:46.516 --> 17:49.526 align:middle
In terms of, is this the-we
believe this is the right move

17:49.526 --> 17:53.746 align:middle
for today, and we think it's-we
think it will serve the three

17:53.746 --> 17:54.936 align:middle
ends that I mentioned.

17:55.846 --> 17:58.756 align:middle
And I've already gone
over how we're thinking

17:58.756 --> 17:59.426 align:middle
about going forward.

17:59.976 --> 18:01.536 align:middle
ANN SAPHIR.

18:02.226 --> 18:05.296 align:middle
Ann Saphir with Reuters.

18:05.296 --> 18:08.286 align:middle
You called it "a midcycle
adjustment to policy."

18:08.766 --> 18:11.356 align:middle
And, I mean, what should
we take this to mean,

18:11.396 --> 18:17.026 align:middle
and what message do you mean
to send with this move today

18:17.136 --> 18:19.046 align:middle
about future rate moves?

18:19.626 --> 18:21.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:21.216 --> 18:23.486 align:middle
Well, the sense of that
is-I mean, that refers back

18:23.486 --> 18:28.576 align:middle
to other times when
the FOMC has cut rates

18:28.576 --> 18:29.546 align:middle
in the middle of a cycle.

18:30.246 --> 18:34.586 align:middle
And I'm contrasting it there
with the beginning-for example,

18:34.586 --> 18:37.186 align:middle
the beginning of a
lengthy cutting cycle.

18:37.186 --> 18:37.366 align:middle
ANN SAPHIR.

18:37.366 --> 18:40.166 align:middle
So we're not at the beginning
of a lengthy cutting cycle?

18:40.166 --> 18:40.496 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:40.496 --> 18:42.326 align:middle
That is not-that's not
what we're seeing now.

18:42.986 --> 18:45.026 align:middle
That's not our perspective
now or outlook.

18:46.456 --> 18:47.576 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

18:48.926 --> 18:52.536 align:middle
Hi there. Are there
any circumstances

18:52.536 --> 18:56.356 align:middle
under which you would
decide to pause-pause

18:56.356 --> 18:59.346 align:middle
at one interest rate cut,
today's interest rate cut,

18:59.346 --> 19:01.446 align:middle
and not-not, not go ahead

19:01.446 --> 19:05.886 align:middle
with further monetary
easing at this stage?

19:05.886 --> 19:11.696 align:middle
Or are you predicting that,
you know, once you've embarked

19:12.846 --> 19:16.036 align:middle
on this easing, you will
have to at least move

19:16.626 --> 19:18.986 align:middle
by one more notch going forward?

19:18.986 --> 19:19.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:19.266 --> 19:22.556 align:middle
You know, so our policy is-will
depend on the implications

19:22.556 --> 19:25.396 align:middle
of incoming data for the
economic outlook as well

19:25.396 --> 19:26.856 align:middle
as evolving risks
to that outlook.

19:27.576 --> 19:31.376 align:middle
And so we're going to be
monitoring the implications

19:31.376 --> 19:35.136 align:middle
of incoming information for
the outlook, as I mentioned.

19:35.376 --> 19:40.956 align:middle
And, so I-that's really where
I would leave you with that.

19:41.346 --> 19:42.386 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

19:44.146 --> 19:45.906 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

19:46.356 --> 19:51.926 align:middle
On capital, Vice Chairman
Quarles has said that the level

19:51.926 --> 19:53.976 align:middle
of capital requirements
that exists right now are

19:53.976 --> 19:55.316 align:middle
such that it's basically

19:55.316 --> 19:58.976 align:middle
like the countercyclical capital
buffer is already turned on,

19:59.306 --> 20:01.376 align:middle
and that he would like
the ability to be able

20:01.376 --> 20:03.186 align:middle
to turn it down in a downturn.

20:03.606 --> 20:05.666 align:middle
I was wondering if
you agreed with that.

20:06.616 --> 20:08.746 align:middle
And then, also, just quickly,

20:08.746 --> 20:12.986 align:middle
on real-time payments-larger
banks have suggested

20:13.046 --> 20:14.796 align:middle
that if the Fed built
its own system,

20:15.206 --> 20:18.496 align:middle
that would be a bait-and-switch,
because the Fed asked-or called

20:18.566 --> 20:19.866 align:middle
for a private-sector system.

20:20.286 --> 20:22.006 align:middle
Do you think that that
is a fair assessment?

20:22.566 --> 20:23.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:23.286 --> 20:27.606 align:middle
Okay. So, on the first, I
would say that I view the level

20:27.606 --> 20:30.476 align:middle
of capital requirements
and the level of capital

20:30.476 --> 20:32.336 align:middle
in the system as
being about right.

20:32.626 --> 20:33.406 align:middle
I do agree with that.

20:34.246 --> 20:36.316 align:middle
The idea that you're talking

20:36.316 --> 20:41.416 align:middle
about is one that's-that Vice
Chair Quarles has talked about.

20:41.416 --> 20:43.416 align:middle
It's a-it's one under
consideration.

20:43.416 --> 20:46.426 align:middle
The idea being, in a
sense, we've chosen,

20:46.656 --> 20:49.646 align:middle
in the United States, to have
high through-the-cycle capital

20:49.646 --> 20:52.486 align:middle
requirements by doubling
the SIFI surcharge,

20:53.466 --> 20:55.476 align:middle
which is the surcharge
that the largest banks have

20:55.546 --> 20:56.796 align:middle
in their capital requirements.

20:57.296 --> 20:59.926 align:middle
We've, in effect, already put

20:59.926 --> 21:03.316 align:middle
in place substantial
countercyclical buffers.

21:04.026 --> 21:06.826 align:middle
And so, conceptually, you can
think of-I'm not saying it's the

21:06.826 --> 21:08.756 align:middle
same thing as a countercyclical
capital buffer.

21:08.756 --> 21:12.556 align:middle
So that's-that's
really the point-is

21:12.556 --> 21:15.396 align:middle
that we don't rely-our
system doesn't rely

21:15.826 --> 21:19.676 align:middle
on our ability-it doesn't
mainly rely on our ability

21:19.676 --> 21:21.766 align:middle
to identify the right
time in the cycle

21:21.766 --> 21:23.936 align:middle
to trigger a countercyclical
tool.

21:24.116 --> 21:27.586 align:middle
We rely on through-the-cycle,
always-on high capital

21:27.586 --> 21:28.436 align:middle
and liquidity requirements.

21:28.436 --> 21:30.496 align:middle
And I think that's
a good thing to do.

21:31.076 --> 21:35.286 align:middle
The idea of putting it in
place so you can cut it,

21:35.286 --> 21:37.096 align:middle
that's something some other
jurisdictions have done,

21:37.096 --> 21:38.026 align:middle
and it's worth considering.

21:38.026 --> 21:39.536 align:middle
I think the United
Kingdom, in particular,

21:39.536 --> 21:42.136 align:middle
has a countercyclical capital
buffer that's always on,

21:42.136 --> 21:45.906 align:middle
but the one point of
it is that you can cut

21:45.906 --> 21:47.636 align:middle
when there's a downturn

21:47.736 --> 21:49.436 align:middle
and therefore give
the banks more room.

21:49.436 --> 21:51.026 align:middle
So this isn't something
we've decided to do.

21:51.026 --> 21:52.786 align:middle
It's just under consideration.

21:53.286 --> 21:55.376 align:middle
In terms of the real-time
payment system,

21:55.676 --> 21:59.886 align:middle
your second question, so this is
something we-the United States

21:59.886 --> 22:02.256 align:middle
is far behind other
countries in terms

22:02.256 --> 22:04.676 align:middle
of having real-time payments
available to the general public.

22:05.416 --> 22:07.016 align:middle
The Fed, really, coming

22:07.016 --> 22:09.776 align:middle
out of the Reserve
System-the Reserve Banks

22:10.306 --> 22:13.566 align:middle
and the Board together-convened
all of the stakeholders

22:13.566 --> 22:16.366 align:middle
around the table to talk
about how we can move forward.

22:16.766 --> 22:19.636 align:middle
This is consumer groups,
technology companies, banks,

22:19.686 --> 22:22.706 align:middle
card companies-pretty
much all of the groups

22:22.706 --> 22:24.136 align:middle
who would be interested-and
worked

22:24.236 --> 22:26.576 align:middle
on a project for several years.

22:27.176 --> 22:29.456 align:middle
And one of the things that came
out of that was a recommendation

22:29.456 --> 22:35.656 align:middle
that the Fed should build a 24
x 7 x 365 real-time settlement

22:35.656 --> 22:37.816 align:middle
system to solve that
problem-to address that problem.

22:38.006 --> 22:41.316 align:middle
We put out a proposal in October
of last year about, you know,

22:41.526 --> 22:44.616 align:middle
should we do this, and we
got quite a lot of comments.

22:44.616 --> 22:46.256 align:middle
They were overwhelmingly
favorable.

22:46.876 --> 22:50.306 align:middle
And I would point out that
in our payment system,

22:50.666 --> 22:53.966 align:middle
in many places, the Fed operates
alongside private-sector

22:53.966 --> 22:56.846 align:middle
operators-for example, in
wholesale payments, in ACH,

22:56.846 --> 22:59.766 align:middle
and in check-so it
wouldn't be unusual or out

22:59.766 --> 23:02.776 align:middle
of keeping with-with how
we've done things in the past.

23:03.246 --> 23:05.546 align:middle
We have not made a
decision on this,

23:05.546 --> 23:07.726 align:middle
but it's something we're
looking at carefully

23:07.726 --> 23:11.026 align:middle
and something I do expect
we'll make a decision on soon.

23:12.626 --> 23:14.626 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

23:15.096 --> 23:16.476 align:middle
Craig Torres from Bloomberg.

23:16.566 --> 23:18.926 align:middle
I'm trying to parse
what you're saying here.

23:18.926 --> 23:21.726 align:middle
On the one hand, you
say the policy tilt

23:21.726 --> 23:24.126 align:middle
in the statement has eased
financial conditions,

23:24.186 --> 23:25.686 align:middle
and that's helping the economy.

23:26.066 --> 23:26.736 align:middle
In that tilt,

23:27.006 --> 23:30.096 align:middle
market participants are
interpreting this language,

23:30.096 --> 23:33.506 align:middle
"will act as appropriate," as
still being in the statement.

23:34.036 --> 23:36.046 align:middle
And, on the other hand,
you say it's not the start

23:36.046 --> 23:38.166 align:middle
of a-of an easing cycle.

23:38.906 --> 23:41.806 align:middle
So what are you saying?

23:41.806 --> 23:43.296 align:middle
Does that mean, you
know, with one

23:43.296 --> 23:45.416 align:middle
or two more cuts you'll be done,

23:45.416 --> 23:47.756 align:middle
and this policy bias comes
out of the statement?

23:48.266 --> 23:50.626 align:middle
Or simply that this
policy bias will come

23:50.626 --> 23:54.016 align:middle
out of the statement sooner
than market participants think?

23:54.216 --> 23:54.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:54.526 --> 23:57.546 align:middle
So it is-as I mentioned,
it's going to depend

23:57.546 --> 24:01.506 align:middle
on the evolving data and
the evolving risk picture.

24:02.046 --> 24:04.726 align:middle
But as we look at the situation
now and the outlook now,

24:04.726 --> 24:09.456 align:middle
what we see is that
it's appropriate

24:09.456 --> 24:11.796 align:middle
to make an adjustment in policy

24:11.836 --> 24:13.586 align:middle
to a somewhat more
accommodative stance.

24:13.586 --> 24:14.446 align:middle
That's what we're seeing.

24:14.916 --> 24:17.096 align:middle
And that's what we're going
to be looking-you know,

24:17.096 --> 24:21.276 align:middle
we'll be looking at incoming
data, at all of the-at the risks

24:21.276 --> 24:22.806 align:middle
that I mentioned,
and the performance

24:22.806 --> 24:24.376 align:middle
of the U.S. economy,
and at low inflation.

24:24.376 --> 24:27.626 align:middle
We'll be looking at that to make
our decisions going forward.

24:28.136 --> 24:30.096 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

24:33.686 --> 24:38.166 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

24:38.166 --> 24:39.106 align:middle
Scott Horsley for NPR.

24:39.506 --> 24:42.386 align:middle
You've talked a number of
times about the people who feel

24:42.386 --> 24:45.006 align:middle
like they are just
recently getting

24:45.006 --> 24:46.976 align:middle
to the punchbowl 10 years
into this expansion.

24:46.976 --> 24:48.246 align:middle
Can you elaborate a little bit

24:48.246 --> 24:51.216 align:middle
on how this rate cut is
expected to help them?

24:51.666 --> 24:52.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:52.116 --> 24:54.476 align:middle
Well, I think the
best thing for people

24:54.476 --> 24:59.936 align:middle
who are-who are feeling that-and
we are getting lots of feedback

24:59.936 --> 25:01.466 align:middle
from people who work
and live in low-

25:01.466 --> 25:03.816 align:middle
and moderate-income
communities to the effect

25:03.876 --> 25:06.226 align:middle
that they're now feeling
the recovery, and, in fact,

25:06.226 --> 25:08.366 align:middle
they haven't felt a better labor
market in anybody's memory,

25:08.366 --> 25:09.476 align:middle
so this is great to hear.

25:10.216 --> 25:12.876 align:middle
And I guess my view is,
the best thing we can do

25:12.876 --> 25:15.776 align:middle
for those people is to sustain
the expansion, keep it going,

25:16.456 --> 25:18.166 align:middle
and that's one

25:18.166 --> 25:20.806 align:middle
of the overarching
goals of-of this move

25:20.806 --> 25:22.026 align:middle
and all of our policy moves.

25:22.386 --> 25:25.876 align:middle
There really is no reason why
the expansion can't keep going.

25:26.256 --> 25:28.016 align:middle
Inflation is not
troublingly high.

25:28.436 --> 25:31.016 align:middle
If you look at the-at the
U.S. economy right now,

25:31.016 --> 25:34.426 align:middle
there's no sector that's booming
and therefore might bust.

25:34.656 --> 25:39.886 align:middle
You have a fairly well-balanced,
in a sense, economy.

25:39.956 --> 25:43.916 align:middle
Now, the engine, though, is
really a consumer economy,

25:43.916 --> 25:45.306 align:middle
which is 70 percent
of the economy.

25:45.306 --> 25:47.726 align:middle
The manufacturing
economy-the investment

25:47.726 --> 25:50.876 align:middle
and manufacturing part
of the economy-is more

25:50.876 --> 25:53.116 align:middle
or less not-not growing much.

25:53.116 --> 25:54.996 align:middle
It's at a healthy level
but not growing much.

25:54.996 --> 25:57.956 align:middle
So-and we hope to help
that with this rate cut.

25:58.006 --> 26:01.546 align:middle
But, I would say, overall, we're
trying to sustain the expansion

26:01.546 --> 26:04.156 align:middle
and keep, you know, close
to our statutory goals,

26:04.156 --> 26:06.066 align:middle
which are maximum
employment and stable prices.

26:07.906 --> 26:09.196 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

26:09.196 --> 26:11.796 align:middle
Chairman Powell,
Donna Borak with CNN.

26:11.796 --> 26:14.736 align:middle
The President has repeatedly
called for this rate hike

26:14.736 --> 26:17.446 align:middle
and for the Fed to end the
runoff of its balance sheet.

26:18.036 --> 26:21.186 align:middle
What do you say to those
that say that the Fed gave

26:21.186 --> 26:22.806 align:middle
into what the President
wanted today?

26:22.806 --> 26:24.846 align:middle
And could you also
elaborate a little bit further

26:24.846 --> 26:27.186 align:middle
on why the Fed decided to speed

26:27.556 --> 26:29.806 align:middle
up its balance sheet runoff
two months earlier today?

26:30.346 --> 26:30.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:30.836 --> 26:37.096 align:middle
So I gave my-my reasons for-our
reasons, really-for doing this.

26:37.756 --> 26:41.186 align:middle
And, you know, just to
touch on that again,

26:41.376 --> 26:44.426 align:middle
this action is designed to
insure against downside risks

26:44.426 --> 26:46.606 align:middle
to the outlook from weak global
growth and trade tensions,

26:47.076 --> 26:48.216 align:middle
offset the negative effect

26:48.216 --> 26:49.686 align:middle
that those factors
are already having,

26:49.686 --> 26:51.696 align:middle
and promote a faster return
of inflation to 2 percent.

26:51.876 --> 26:54.066 align:middle
That's what we've been
talking about all year long,

26:54.776 --> 26:58.056 align:middle
and we've gradually moved
our policy in the direction

26:58.056 --> 26:58.866 align:middle
of more accommodation.

26:59.026 --> 27:00.376 align:middle
I think what you
see is an economy

27:00.376 --> 27:02.426 align:middle
that has reacted well to that.

27:02.426 --> 27:05.256 align:middle
So that's what we're doing,
and that's why we're doing it.

27:05.256 --> 27:07.936 align:middle
We never take into account
political considerations.

27:07.936 --> 27:09.636 align:middle
There's no place in our
discussions for that.

27:09.946 --> 27:12.086 align:middle
We also don't conduct
monetary policy in order

27:12.086 --> 27:13.096 align:middle
to prove our independence.

27:13.626 --> 27:18.466 align:middle
We conduct monetary policy
in order to-to move as close

27:18.466 --> 27:20.276 align:middle
as possible to our
statutory goals,

27:20.276 --> 27:21.866 align:middle
and that's what we're
always going to do.

27:22.016 --> 27:23.596 align:middle
We're always going to
use our tools that way.

27:23.596 --> 27:26.226 align:middle
And then, at the
end, we'll-you know,

27:26.226 --> 27:27.626 align:middle
we'll live with the results.

27:28.326 --> 27:31.876 align:middle
In terms of the balance sheet,
that was really just a matter

27:31.876 --> 27:33.796 align:middle
of simplicity and consistency.

27:33.846 --> 27:39.226 align:middle
Really nothing more
to it than that.

27:39.506 --> 27:40.236 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

27:42.156 --> 27:43.116 align:middle
Thank you, Chairman Powell.

27:43.406 --> 27:44.846 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

27:45.136 --> 27:48.296 align:middle
I was wondering-we
weren't in the room,

27:48.296 --> 27:50.356 align:middle
but I think it's a
fair assumption to know

27:50.356 --> 27:53.216 align:middle
that the two dissenters
probably spoke

27:53.216 --> 27:55.296 align:middle
about financial stability
concerns.

27:55.296 --> 27:58.606 align:middle
So I was wondering
if you could talk

27:58.606 --> 28:02.246 align:middle
about what you-what was
your response to them

28:02.426 --> 28:04.846 align:middle
when those concerns were raised?

28:05.196 --> 28:07.336 align:middle
I've collected a couple
of things from the IMF,

28:07.546 --> 28:09.986 align:middle
and the Bank of International
Settlements said

28:09.986 --> 28:13.216 align:middle
that it's just-when you have low
rates, you just get more debt

28:13.446 --> 28:16.256 align:middle
in the economy, and that
there's also this feeling

28:16.256 --> 28:18.286 align:middle
that it makes it
harder to raise rates.

28:18.776 --> 28:19.666 align:middle
So could you talk about that?

28:19.876 --> 28:20.196 align:middle
Thank you.

28:20.546 --> 28:20.966 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:20.966 --> 28:23.366 align:middle
Yes. So, first, let me
say-I'll just speak for myself,

28:23.396 --> 28:26.736 align:middle
but I understand those
concerns very well.

28:27.366 --> 28:29.586 align:middle
I do. I've-you know,
I've studied them,

28:29.586 --> 28:31.846 align:middle
I've spoken about them, and
I take them very seriously.

28:32.296 --> 28:36.056 align:middle
But as I look at today's
situation, I don't see them

28:36.056 --> 28:38.326 align:middle
as a reason not to
take this action today.

28:38.326 --> 28:41.106 align:middle
I just don't think-that
would be my point.

28:41.106 --> 28:44.966 align:middle
And one of the reasons
why I think that is,

28:45.036 --> 28:47.296 align:middle
if you look-if you look-so
we have a financial stability

28:47.296 --> 28:48.916 align:middle
framework now for
the first time.

28:48.916 --> 28:51.146 align:middle
Before the crisis,
we didn't have this,

28:51.146 --> 28:52.576 align:middle
but now we have it,
and we publish it.

28:52.576 --> 28:54.566 align:middle
And we look at, really,
four big things

28:55.156 --> 28:56.396 align:middle
so that we know we can-you know,

28:56.706 --> 28:57.926 align:middle
the public can hold
us accountable

28:57.926 --> 29:01.206 align:middle
and compare us meeting
to meeting, and-you know,

29:01.206 --> 29:03.016 align:middle
and see whether we
got this right,

29:03.066 --> 29:04.196 align:middle
because it's transparent now.

29:04.266 --> 29:06.546 align:middle
But the four things we look
at are valuation pressures.

29:06.896 --> 29:10.866 align:middle
And we do see notable valuation
pressures in some markets but,

29:11.266 --> 29:14.226 align:middle
you know, honestly, not at
a highly troubling level.

29:14.626 --> 29:16.816 align:middle
In terms of household
borrowing, household

29:16.816 --> 29:18.236 align:middle
and business borrowing
is the second thing.

29:18.446 --> 29:20.676 align:middle
Households are in a
very good shape overall.

29:21.156 --> 29:23.296 align:middle
I'll come back to
business borrowing.

29:23.566 --> 29:26.306 align:middle
Leverage in the financial system
is low, and funding risk is low.

29:26.306 --> 29:29.906 align:middle
So, overall, staff's view has
been-and my view has been-that

29:29.906 --> 29:32.496 align:middle
if you look overall, financial
stability vulnerabilities

29:32.586 --> 29:33.306 align:middle
are moderate.

29:34.136 --> 29:36.896 align:middle
The place that gets all the
attention right now-a lot

29:36.896 --> 29:38.516 align:middle
of attention-is business
borrowing,

29:39.146 --> 29:41.036 align:middle
and we look very
carefully at that.

29:41.036 --> 29:43.136 align:middle
What's happening with
business borrowing is,

29:43.676 --> 29:47.666 align:middle
the loans have moved off the
balance sheets of the banks

29:47.666 --> 29:51.686 align:middle
and into market-based vehicles,
which tend to be stably funded.

29:52.366 --> 29:53.966 align:middle
But, nonetheless, it's clear

29:53.966 --> 29:56.346 align:middle
that the highly leveraged
business sector could act

29:56.346 --> 29:57.916 align:middle
as an amplifier to a downturn.

29:57.916 --> 29:59.486 align:middle
So we're watching
that very carefully.

29:59.996 --> 30:02.236 align:middle
But, again, I think
if you look overall

30:02.236 --> 30:07.036 align:middle
at the U.S. financial system,
what you see is a high level

30:07.036 --> 30:08.666 align:middle
of resilience-much higher

30:08.666 --> 30:10.886 align:middle
than it was before the
crisis-and that's something

30:10.886 --> 30:11.646 align:middle
to take comfort from.

30:11.646 --> 30:13.726 align:middle
And I think all of that
gives us the ability

30:13.936 --> 30:18.726 align:middle
to use monetary policy for its
purposes and rely on supervisory

30:18.726 --> 30:21.046 align:middle
and regulatory tools
to-you know,

30:21.046 --> 30:22.626 align:middle
to keep the financial
system resilient.

30:23.826 --> 30:26.026 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

30:26.416 --> 30:30.376 align:middle
So you're not doing something
today for-to help today,

30:30.376 --> 30:32.356 align:middle
and then it's going to cause
problems down the road?

30:32.506 --> 30:34.276 align:middle
You're not worried about
that kind of dynamic?

30:34.746 --> 30:34.956 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:35.666 --> 30:38.176 align:middle
There are very few things that
I don't worry about at all.

30:39.086 --> 30:41.376 align:middle
So, of course, of
course, we monitor.

30:41.376 --> 30:45.016 align:middle
We have-every quarter we
have an extensive briefing

30:45.016 --> 30:48.186 align:middle
on financial stability,
and we had that yesterday.

30:48.306 --> 30:50.696 align:middle
So we look at this
on an ongoing basis.

30:50.696 --> 30:51.736 align:middle
We have a great team.

30:52.246 --> 30:56.106 align:middle
We liaise with central
banks around the world.

30:56.106 --> 30:57.676 align:middle
You know, one of my
colleagues is the head

30:57.676 --> 31:01.096 align:middle
of the Financial Stability
Board globally, so we're very,

31:01.256 --> 31:03.646 align:middle
very much monitoring
these things all the time.

31:04.456 --> 31:05.936 align:middle
And we worry about
them all the time.

31:05.936 --> 31:07.966 align:middle
We're always looking at
the-we're looking for that thing

31:08.006 --> 31:11.066 align:middle
that we may have missed
a lot of the time.

31:11.396 --> 31:12.376 align:middle
But the things we
haven't missed,

31:12.376 --> 31:14.556 align:middle
I think they paint
a mixed picture,

31:14.556 --> 31:16.416 align:middle
but not one that
should prevent us

31:16.416 --> 31:18.816 align:middle
from taking monetary
policy actions

31:18.816 --> 31:24.796 align:middle
that we think are appropriate
to support the economy.

31:27.516 --> 31:28.406 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

31:28.406 --> 31:29.436 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger, AP.

31:29.436 --> 31:30.766 align:middle
You've talked in
this press conference

31:30.766 --> 31:32.896 align:middle
about being data
dependent going forward,

31:33.206 --> 31:36.066 align:middle
and that this is not the start
of a series of rate cuts.

31:36.436 --> 31:41.456 align:middle
But the financial markets seem
to think that this is the start

31:41.456 --> 31:42.996 align:middle
of a series of rate cuts,

31:42.996 --> 31:45.406 align:middle
and they're predicting
three, four cuts this year.

31:45.766 --> 31:48.876 align:middle
Is this your effort to try
to damp down that-that thing?

31:48.876 --> 31:49.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:49.116 --> 31:50.146 align:middle
No. Let me be clear.

31:50.266 --> 31:52.366 align:middle
What I said was, it's
not-it's not the beginning

31:52.366 --> 31:54.006 align:middle
of a long series of rate cuts.

31:54.006 --> 31:56.696 align:middle
I didn't-I didn't say it's
just one or anything like that.

31:56.696 --> 31:59.046 align:middle
What I said is, when you think
about rate-cutting cycles,

31:59.046 --> 32:00.796 align:middle
they go on for a long time,

32:00.796 --> 32:03.786 align:middle
and the Committee is not
seeing that-not seeing us

32:03.786 --> 32:04.246 align:middle
in that place.

32:04.246 --> 32:07.116 align:middle
You would do that if you
saw real economic weakness

32:07.536 --> 32:09.866 align:middle
and you thought that the
federal funds rate needed

32:09.866 --> 32:10.986 align:middle
to be cut a lot.

32:11.586 --> 32:12.556 align:middle
That's not what we're seeing.

32:12.556 --> 32:13.326 align:middle
That's not what we're seeing.

32:13.326 --> 32:17.746 align:middle
What we're seeing is that it's
appropriate to-to adjust policy

32:17.746 --> 32:21.216 align:middle
to a somewhat more
accommodative stance over time,

32:21.286 --> 32:23.206 align:middle
and that's how we're
looking at it.

32:23.206 --> 32:28.256 align:middle
What I said was, it's not a long
cutting cycle-in other words,

32:28.256 --> 32:30.236 align:middle
referring to what we do
when there's a recession

32:30.236 --> 32:31.716 align:middle
or a very severe downturn.

32:31.716 --> 32:33.866 align:middle
That's-that's really
what I was ruling out.

32:34.896 --> 32:38.716 align:middle
I think, if you look back at
other midcycle adjustments,

32:38.716 --> 32:42.406 align:middle
you'll see-you know, I
don't know that they'll be,

32:42.686 --> 32:43.846 align:middle
in the end, comparable or not,

32:43.846 --> 32:45.126 align:middle
but you'll see examples
of these.

32:46.846 --> 32:49.636 align:middle
DON LEE. Don Lee
with the L.A. Times.

32:50.356 --> 32:54.476 align:middle
You mentioned the difficulty
of assessing trade tensions

32:54.476 --> 32:55.686 align:middle
in the economic outlook.

32:57.466 --> 32:58.726 align:middle
Could you say how much

32:58.726 --> 33:02.356 align:middle
of a factor the U.S.-China
trade conflict was

33:02.356 --> 33:05.346 align:middle
in the Fed's decision
to cut rates?

33:05.406 --> 33:08.516 align:middle
And if the current
stalemate and the threat

33:08.516 --> 33:13.976 align:middle
of more tariffs continue,
what would that mean

33:13.976 --> 33:18.296 align:middle
for future interest
rates and possible cuts?

33:18.296 --> 33:19.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:19.116 --> 33:21.226 align:middle
So, you know, I wouldn't
bring it

33:21.226 --> 33:24.566 align:middle
down to any one trade
thing or any one factor.

33:24.566 --> 33:26.966 align:middle
I think we look at a
broad range of factors,

33:27.536 --> 33:31.216 align:middle
and trade uncertainty-trade
policy uncertainty is one

33:31.216 --> 33:31.406 align:middle
of them.

33:31.406 --> 33:33.586 align:middle
That certainly includes
the discussions with China,

33:33.936 --> 33:35.006 align:middle
but I wouldn't-I
wouldn't be able

33:35.006 --> 33:36.876 align:middle
to tell you how much
of it is due to that.

33:37.476 --> 33:40.166 align:middle
And without knowing-you
know, I think we-with trade,

33:40.166 --> 33:41.726 align:middle
we have to react to
the developments,

33:41.726 --> 33:42.676 align:middle
and we don't know
what they'll be,

33:43.236 --> 33:46.206 align:middle
and so it's hard to exactly say.

33:46.946 --> 33:48.896 align:middle
Certainly, we've seen, though,

33:48.896 --> 33:50.896 align:middle
that when there's a
sharp confrontation

33:51.136 --> 33:54.326 align:middle
between two large
economies, you can see effects

33:54.326 --> 33:55.816 align:middle
on business confidence
pretty quickly

33:55.816 --> 33:57.116 align:middle
and on financial
markets pretty quickly.

33:57.116 --> 33:57.966 align:middle
We saw that in June.

33:58.106 --> 34:01.226 align:middle
But then we saw them unwind
after that, to some extent.

34:01.226 --> 34:06.206 align:middle
You've seen it returning to-to a
much lower temperature, I think.

34:06.646 --> 34:10.226 align:middle
Again, with-with trade policy,
we're just going to be watching

34:10.316 --> 34:12.486 align:middle
and trying to assess
the implications

34:12.486 --> 34:14.146 align:middle
for the-for the U.S. outlook.

34:14.146 --> 34:16.736 align:middle
DON LEE. How much of an
effect could the tariffs have?

34:17.686 --> 34:17.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:17.986 --> 34:20.876 align:middle
You know, the mechanical effects
of the tariffs are quite small.

34:20.876 --> 34:23.826 align:middle
They're not large as it
relates to the U.S. economy.

34:23.936 --> 34:27.666 align:middle
The real question is, what
are the effects on the economy

34:27.666 --> 34:30.026 align:middle
through the-through the
confidence channel-business

34:30.026 --> 34:30.836 align:middle
confidence channel?

34:30.836 --> 34:34.276 align:middle
And, again, very, very
hard to tease that out.

34:34.276 --> 34:36.756 align:middle
I've seen some research,
which-you know,

34:36.756 --> 34:40.926 align:middle
which says that they are
meaningful-meaningful effects

34:40.926 --> 34:41.996 align:middle
on-on output.

34:43.116 --> 34:44.766 align:middle
That's to say, not trivial.

34:45.256 --> 34:48.166 align:middle
And I think that that sounds
right, but it's quite hard

34:48.166 --> 34:50.116 align:middle
to get-there is no way to
get an accurate measure.

34:50.116 --> 34:51.686 align:middle
You have to look at
a range of estimates,

34:51.686 --> 34:55.156 align:middle
and I think businesses will
tell you that it's a factor,

34:55.156 --> 34:57.576 align:middle
particularly businesses that
have-manufacturing businesses

34:57.576 --> 34:58.556 align:middle
that have supply chains

34:58.556 --> 35:01.926 align:middle
that cross international
borders will all tell you

35:01.926 --> 35:02.756 align:middle
that it's been a challenge.

35:02.756 --> 35:04.516 align:middle
Many of them have made
adjustments, and they've gotten

35:04.516 --> 35:08.766 align:middle
to a place where it's okay,
but it's-it's been a challenge.

35:11.116 --> 35:14.006 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

35:16.876 --> 35:19.376 align:middle
Paul Kiernan from
Dow Jones Newswires.

35:19.376 --> 35:20.736 align:middle
Thanks for the question,
Chairman.

35:20.796 --> 35:24.586 align:middle
As the press conference-as
this press conference has gone

35:24.766 --> 35:27.526 align:middle
under way, markets
have declined.

35:27.526 --> 35:32.396 align:middle
The Dow is down as
much as 400 points.

35:32.706 --> 35:37.366 align:middle
What I'm hearing is a reluctance
to provide more guidance

35:37.526 --> 35:39.836 align:middle
around the future path of rates.

35:40.766 --> 35:44.276 align:middle
And I'm wondering if that
reflects a greater lack

35:44.276 --> 35:46.266 align:middle
of consensus on the Committee.

35:46.266 --> 35:51.226 align:middle
And, you know, how much
consensus do you want to see

35:51.226 --> 35:54.596 align:middle
around these decisions, and how
split are people about this?

35:54.596 --> 35:55.096 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:55.326 --> 35:56.286 align:middle
You know, you're right.

35:56.286 --> 35:58.676 align:middle
There's a range of
views on the Committee,

35:58.676 --> 36:01.726 align:middle
and-but the Committee is
unified, completely unified

36:02.616 --> 36:06.816 align:middle
on our dedication to making
the best policy decisions

36:06.816 --> 36:07.476 align:middle
that we can make.

36:07.626 --> 36:09.536 align:middle
And that means people
have a responsibility

36:09.536 --> 36:11.596 align:middle
to do their best thinking
and to present that thinking,

36:11.596 --> 36:12.946 align:middle
and I wouldn't have
it any other way.

36:13.866 --> 36:18.696 align:middle
In terms of the way forward, we
will be monitoring the factors

36:18.696 --> 36:22.586 align:middle
that I mentioned, and we laid
that out in the-you know,

36:22.586 --> 36:24.006 align:middle
in the postmeeting statement,

36:24.006 --> 36:25.136 align:middle
and that's the roadmap
we're going

36:25.136 --> 36:26.236 align:middle
to be following going forward.

36:26.236 --> 36:27.746 align:middle
We're going to be
data dependent.

36:27.746 --> 36:33.946 align:middle
We're going to be, as we
always are, doing what we need

36:33.946 --> 36:35.406 align:middle
to do-what we believe
we need to do

36:35.446 --> 36:37.366 align:middle
to support the economic
expansion.

36:39.696 --> 36:40.846 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

36:43.676 --> 36:45.596 align:middle
Hi, John Heltman
with American Banker.

36:45.676 --> 36:47.916 align:middle
So it's been about 18 months

36:47.946 --> 36:50.106 align:middle
since the Fed issued
its enforcement action

36:50.106 --> 36:51.096 align:middle
against Wells Fargo.

36:51.516 --> 36:55.986 align:middle
And I was wondering if you
could characterize the progress

36:56.036 --> 36:58.426 align:middle
that the bank has made towards
the shortcomings that it had

36:58.426 --> 37:00.256 align:middle
in its risk-management
processes.

37:00.706 --> 37:04.736 align:middle
And I'm also curious
whether their lack

37:04.736 --> 37:09.796 align:middle
of a permanent CEO has
hampered progress, in your eyes.

37:09.796 --> 37:10.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:10.126 --> 37:16.836 align:middle
So the problems at Wells
Fargo-that arose at Wells Fargo

37:17.006 --> 37:21.686 align:middle
around risk management and
consumer-the way they dealt

37:21.686 --> 37:24.296 align:middle
with the consumer were
actually pretty deep,

37:24.926 --> 37:27.326 align:middle
and I think the company
realizes that.

37:27.456 --> 37:28.926 align:middle
And they're not going

37:28.926 --> 37:31.886 align:middle
to be fixed-they haven't been
fixed quickly, and, frankly,

37:31.886 --> 37:34.316 align:middle
we didn't expect them
to be fixed quickly.

37:34.316 --> 37:38.726 align:middle
So they're-you know, they-they
will be under the growth cap,

37:39.126 --> 37:43.626 align:middle
our enforcement action, until
the Board votes to lift it,

37:44.566 --> 37:48.616 align:middle
and that's not something we're
considering doing right now.

37:48.616 --> 37:50.906 align:middle
And the Committee-I mean,
the company is working away

37:50.996 --> 37:53.706 align:middle
to address these
issues, but, as I said,

37:53.706 --> 37:55.166 align:middle
they're deep-seated issues,

37:55.166 --> 37:59.826 align:middle
and it just takes
time to address them.

38:00.136 --> 38:02.376 align:middle
I wouldn't comment
on the CEO question.

38:02.576 --> 38:05.596 align:middle
I don't really have
anything for you on that.

38:05.596 --> 38:05.906 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

38:05.906 --> 38:08.426 align:middle
I mean, I just-I'd like to
clarify, like, are you pleased

38:08.426 --> 38:10.336 align:middle
with the responsiveness
you're getting from them?

38:10.336 --> 38:11.936 align:middle
Do you feel like
this is going the way

38:11.936 --> 38:12.806 align:middle
that you kind of
wanted it to go?

38:12.806 --> 38:13.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:13.016 --> 38:14.036 align:middle
I'm not going to
characterize it.

38:14.036 --> 38:15.356 align:middle
You know-I mean, I
have characterized it.

38:15.356 --> 38:17.746 align:middle
We have-we have an
enforcement action in place.

38:18.086 --> 38:20.496 align:middle
The company is working
away at addressing it.

38:20.986 --> 38:22.366 align:middle
They take it seriously.

38:22.366 --> 38:23.966 align:middle
I think they do see it as we do,

38:23.966 --> 38:25.526 align:middle
as something that
has to go deep.

38:26.206 --> 38:29.826 align:middle
And, you know, we'll lift the
growth cap when we're satisfied.

38:30.906 --> 38:32.066 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

38:32.306 --> 38:34.106 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

38:34.476 --> 38:36.996 align:middle
So when we have our
next recession,

38:37.486 --> 38:42.426 align:middle
the Fed will have less room-it
will happen-the Fed will have

38:42.456 --> 38:45.436 align:middle
less room to maneuver
cutting interest rates

38:45.436 --> 38:46.526 align:middle
since you're cutting now.

38:46.906 --> 38:48.586 align:middle
How big of a problem
will that be?

38:49.656 --> 38:50.396 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:50.436 --> 38:54.616 align:middle
You know, the premise is not-I
will question your premise

38:54.616 --> 38:55.916 align:middle
for a second.

38:56.266 --> 39:01.276 align:middle
If you remember whether-again,
one of the purposes

39:01.276 --> 39:05.706 align:middle
of our-of our cut today is
to support the expansion.

39:06.466 --> 39:11.216 align:middle
And we don't know when-and
if it really works-if

39:11.216 --> 39:13.466 align:middle
that works very well and the
economy gets going again,

39:13.766 --> 39:16.966 align:middle
you know, you don't know
where the-where the funds,

39:16.966 --> 39:19.366 align:middle
in other cycles-and I don't know
whether this will happen or not,

39:19.366 --> 39:22.486 align:middle
but in other cycles, the Fed
wound up raising rates again

39:22.486 --> 39:24.236 align:middle
after a midcycle adjustment.

39:24.236 --> 39:28.756 align:middle
Again, I'm not predicting that,
but I don't think that we know

39:28.756 --> 39:30.656 align:middle
that we won't have-that
we'll have less ammo

39:30.656 --> 39:31.696 align:middle
because of these things.

39:32.576 --> 39:33.186 align:middle
That's one thing.

39:33.466 --> 39:36.396 align:middle
So- NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

39:36.556 --> 39:40.106 align:middle
But you won't be able to cut
as much if-if rates are low.

39:40.106 --> 39:43.626 align:middle
I mean, you will have-have
less ammo, in that sense.

39:43.626 --> 39:44.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:44.216 --> 39:45.386 align:middle
Well, but you're assuming

39:45.386 --> 39:47.096 align:middle
that we would never
raise rates again,

39:47.096 --> 39:48.346 align:middle
that once we've cut these rates

39:48.346 --> 39:49.826 align:middle
that they can never
go back up again.

39:49.826 --> 39:53.506 align:middle
Just as a matter of principle,
I don't think that's right.

39:53.856 --> 39:56.386 align:middle
In other-in other
long, long cycles,

39:56.386 --> 40:00.636 align:middle
long U.S. business cycles have
sometimes involved this kind

40:00.636 --> 40:04.046 align:middle
of event where the Fed
will stop hiking-in fact,

40:04.046 --> 40:06.146 align:middle
will cut-and then will
go back to hiking.

40:06.146 --> 40:07.896 align:middle
Again, I don't know whether
that'll happen right here.

40:07.896 --> 40:10.406 align:middle
It doesn't seem-it doesn't seem

40:10.646 --> 40:13.996 align:middle
like something that's in-you
know, particularly likely,

40:13.996 --> 40:15.706 align:middle
frankly, but we don't know that.

40:15.706 --> 40:20.816 align:middle
And the other thing is, I
think by extending the cycle,

40:21.336 --> 40:25.786 align:middle
you-you do have a lot
of benefits from that.

40:25.786 --> 40:29.926 align:middle
And I think, you know, we will
use the tools that we have.

40:29.926 --> 40:31.836 align:middle
A couple of rate hikes one
way or the other isn't going

40:31.836 --> 40:33.856 align:middle
to matter so much if
there is a downturn.

40:33.856 --> 40:35.366 align:middle
And you're right, there
will eventually be one.

40:35.766 --> 40:37.876 align:middle
We'll use all of our
tools aggressively,

40:38.106 --> 40:39.916 align:middle
as we need to, when
that time comes.

40:41.356 --> 40:43.756 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

40:45.116 --> 40:46.816 align:middle
Hi, Jean Yung with MNI.

40:47.426 --> 40:50.956 align:middle
Can you give us an update
on the ongoing reassessment

40:50.956 --> 40:53.226 align:middle
of the inflation framework?

40:53.226 --> 40:54.796 align:middle
And have the discussions

40:54.796 --> 40:58.186 align:middle
so far this year had any
bearing on today's decision?

40:58.636 --> 40:59.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:59.286 --> 41:04.816 align:middle
So the monetary policy review
is really for-it's really there

41:04.816 --> 41:09.346 align:middle
to look at the way we make
policy in the longer run.

41:09.346 --> 41:11.196 align:middle
It's not something
that enters directly

41:11.196 --> 41:12.516 align:middle
into our discussions today.

41:12.756 --> 41:15.676 align:middle
We've had-so far we've
had a series of meetings,

41:15.676 --> 41:18.796 align:middle
called Fed Listens, at
almost all the Reserve Banks.

41:18.796 --> 41:20.256 align:middle
Soon it will be all
the Reserve Banks.

41:20.816 --> 41:23.136 align:middle
And we meet there with the
constituencies that we serve,

41:23.136 --> 41:25.656 align:middle
and they've been
very, very successful,

41:26.696 --> 41:30.206 align:middle
hearing from people-not
just-not just economists,

41:30.206 --> 41:32.106 align:middle
but people who are
not economists,

41:32.106 --> 41:34.776 align:middle
how-how their lives
interact with the Fed's work.

41:34.776 --> 41:36.486 align:middle
It's been-it's been great.

41:36.486 --> 41:39.506 align:middle
I've got to say, it's been even
better than I hoped it would be.

41:39.786 --> 41:43.096 align:middle
We're just now beginning the
process of incorporating all

41:43.096 --> 41:46.136 align:middle
of that feedback, and we're
going to be having a series

41:46.136 --> 41:50.416 align:middle
of meetings, beginning today
and yesterday, as we evaluate,

41:50.606 --> 41:51.536 align:middle
you know, the questions

41:51.536 --> 41:52.726 align:middle
that we're asking
about our framework.

41:52.726 --> 41:55.356 align:middle
So it's very early to say where
that's going, but, you know,

41:55.356 --> 41:58.886 align:middle
we're setting the table now and
looking at what our framework is

41:58.886 --> 42:00.256 align:middle
and looking at how
it's performed,

42:00.636 --> 42:02.636 align:middle
looking at how all
frameworks performed

42:02.686 --> 42:04.926 align:middle
around the world during the
Global Financial Crisis.

42:05.416 --> 42:06.996 align:middle
I expect we'll be
at this a while.

42:07.386 --> 42:08.466 align:middle
I'm very excited about it.

42:08.466 --> 42:11.216 align:middle
I think it's been
a good exercise.

42:11.216 --> 42:17.426 align:middle
I think it's opened us up
to sunlight and perspectives

42:17.426 --> 42:19.006 align:middle
that we might not
have gotten otherwise.

42:19.006 --> 42:19.976 align:middle
And I think it's a good time.

42:20.006 --> 42:22.106 align:middle
You know, you have-10
years after the crisis,

42:22.106 --> 42:24.906 align:middle
you're really living in a
new normal for the economy

42:24.906 --> 42:27.496 align:middle
and monetary policy, and it's
a really good time to step back

42:27.496 --> 42:30.386 align:middle
and ask whether there are
some things that we can do

42:30.386 --> 42:32.366 align:middle
to improve our framework.

42:33.926 --> 42:35.056 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

42:37.746 --> 42:38.446 align:middle
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

42:38.446 --> 42:40.026 align:middle
Courtenay Brown from Axios.

42:40.026 --> 42:41.576 align:middle
Can you give us a sense

42:41.576 --> 42:44.196 align:middle
of whether the Committee
feels constrained at all

42:44.196 --> 42:46.236 align:middle
by the market's expectations
for more cuts

42:46.686 --> 42:49.166 align:middle
or other developments
in financial markets?

42:49.536 --> 42:50.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:51.126 --> 42:56.236 align:middle
So I think this was-this
was well telegraphed.

42:56.236 --> 42:58.646 align:middle
What we did today
was-was very consistent

42:58.646 --> 43:02.056 align:middle
with what we had said
we were going to do.

43:02.736 --> 43:05.456 align:middle
I had mentioned the
reasons for it.

43:05.456 --> 43:07.416 align:middle
They've been well telegraphed,
and I think-you know,

43:07.416 --> 43:11.016 align:middle
I think they will-they
will achieve their goals.

43:11.636 --> 43:14.846 align:middle
We do know that monetary policy
works through communications

43:15.046 --> 43:16.986 align:middle
and then actions
that are consistent

43:16.986 --> 43:20.656 align:middle
with those communications,
and we think that the changes

43:20.656 --> 43:22.156 align:middle
that we've made this
year have really worked.

43:22.286 --> 43:24.336 align:middle
Of course, we always
retain the flexibility

43:24.386 --> 43:27.946 align:middle
to adjust our communications
and our actions to-in light

43:27.946 --> 43:29.746 align:middle
of incoming data and the
evolving risk picture,

43:30.486 --> 43:33.226 align:middle
but-I'll leave it there.

43:35.176 --> 43:35.966 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

43:36.056 --> 43:37.686 align:middle
Hi, Brian Cheung
with Yahoo Finance.

43:37.806 --> 43:39.696 align:middle
Just to expand on that point
about communication, I mean,

43:39.826 --> 43:41.776 align:middle
we saw that markets have
been particularly sensitive

43:41.776 --> 43:43.976 align:middle
with regards to New York Fed
President John Williams's

43:43.976 --> 43:46.336 align:middle
remarks before the
blackout period.

43:46.626 --> 43:49.766 align:middle
So the Fed added language
in the statement that says

43:49.766 --> 43:51.586 align:middle
that the Committee is
contemplating the future path

43:51.586 --> 43:52.906 align:middle
of the target range for
the federal funds rate

43:52.906 --> 43:54.296 align:middle
as it monitors implications.

43:54.766 --> 43:57.696 align:middle
I'm wondering-now there's
kind of this inflection point

43:57.696 --> 44:00.176 align:middle
about whether or not
the downside risk,

44:00.176 --> 44:03.016 align:middle
because you've had to cut
rates, outweighs the fact

44:03.016 --> 44:05.896 align:middle
that you still see
a positive outlook

44:05.896 --> 44:07.626 align:middle
of the U.S. economy
at a baseline.

44:07.966 --> 44:10.356 align:middle
So, just wondering if you can
kind of clarify all those things

44:10.356 --> 44:12.226 align:middle
within the context of the
challenges of communicating it.

44:12.786 --> 44:13.696 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:13.696 --> 44:17.576 align:middle
Yeah, so again, I see the U.S.
outlook as being a positive one.

44:17.766 --> 44:20.706 align:middle
And we do-we have
had these global,

44:20.706 --> 44:22.586 align:middle
really, risks to the outlook.

44:22.586 --> 44:25.336 align:middle
There really-there really is
nothing in the U.S. economy

44:25.746 --> 44:27.506 align:middle
that presents a-you know,

44:27.506 --> 44:29.776 align:middle
a prominent near-term
threat to the U.S. economy.

44:29.776 --> 44:33.876 align:middle
As I mentioned, there's
no-there's no segment that's

44:33.876 --> 44:37.006 align:middle
really-or sector that's really
boiling over and overheating.

44:37.006 --> 44:37.776 align:middle
Nothing like that.

44:37.776 --> 44:40.596 align:middle
It's-it's within-within
the economy.

44:41.136 --> 44:43.746 align:middle
It's-it's healthy,
so I would say that.

44:44.266 --> 44:46.136 align:middle
Downside risks are
really coming from abroad.

44:46.136 --> 44:48.576 align:middle
And, of course, we are concerned
about-about low inflation.

44:49.266 --> 44:51.726 align:middle
But-and, by the way, those risks

44:51.726 --> 44:54.296 align:middle
from abroad are affecting
the manufacturing sector here

44:54.296 --> 44:59.166 align:middle
and business investment-fixed
investment.

45:06.286 --> 45:08.936 align:middle
So-thank you.

